  "why": "The statement asks, for each n, for one assignment of a natural-number sequence to every real x, with an eventually periodic sequence exactly when x is algebraic of degree n. Each n is an instance, so the classical n=1 and n=2 results are cases, both from long before the snapshot. Everything for n=3 departs from the statement's form. The inputs are conjugate vectors or minimal-polynomial data (Murru, Karpenkov), there is a family of algorithms (Dasaratha, Jacobs), or the setting is p-adic or about transcendence. Those are variants, not cases, and that was the closest call: Karpenkov's totally real result could be read as a case, but it needs a vector of conjugates rather than a single real x, and the record never shows the converse for single real inputs. The Monatshefte DOI has only a year, 2024, so its date cannot be placed. Tagnon Part I says it proves no general theorem and certifies finitely many polynomials and fields, so it is instances. Part II is conditional on an unproved hypothesis (C_kappa), which is a reduction, so none. Both Tagnon papers disclose unnamed AI assistance for 'exploratory computations and manuscript preparation'. No system is recorded and no AI-produced mathematics is identified, so the AI role is none. Barclay's claim has no locatable text and is contradicted by later work, so it is not credible.",
